<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 5</h2>
<p style="text-align: center;"><em>Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</em></p>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
  <tr>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 5</td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Mediation</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> Pre-K (CEFR: A1.1)</td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> My Family</td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> This Is Me!</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
      <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Mediation: Text: Mimic simple actions shown in picture books.</p>
      <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Mediation: Can act out family roles from picture books or stories (e.g., pretending to be "dad").</p>
      <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Can use gestures to describe family members (e.g., showing "tall," "short," "sad," or "happy").</p>
      <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Can use mimicking with drawings to represent family members for peers (e.g., drawing a picture of the mother and showing it to peers while saying the word "mother").</p>
    </td>
  </tr>
</table>

<h3 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h3>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; color: #1a5276; width: 33%;">Stage & Description</th>
      <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; color: #1a5276; width: 34%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
      <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; color: #1a5276; width: 33%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher leads a "Family Members Song" (e.g., to the tune of "The Wheels on the Bus"). Students sing and use simple TPR actions for "mom" (hug), "dad" (strong pose), "sister" (twirl), "brother" (jump), "baby" (rock). This activates vocabulary and encourages movement. Emphasis on respecting family roles through actions.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher holds up large flashcards of "mom," "dad," "brother," "sister," "baby." The teacher points to each and says, "This is mom," "This is dad," etc., with clear pronunciation, especially of /m/ in "mom" and /s/ in "sister." The teacher also models gestures for "happy" (smile) and "sad" (frown) along with saying the words.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
        <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Teacher shows a picture of a dad) "Is this mom or dad?" (Students point or say "dad")</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Teacher shows a happy face picture) "Is she happy or sad?" (Students point or say "happy")</li>
        </ul>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "My Family".</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A simple, large picture book titled "My Happy Family" featuring illustrations of a family doing various actions (e.g., mom cooking, dad playing, brother reading, sister singing, baby sleeping). The book should explicitly show family members and their moods.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Students will understand and respond to simple questions about family members and their actions/feelings presented visually.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b> The teacher reads the book, pointing to the pictures and naming family members and their actions. For each page:</p>
        <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Who is it?" (pointing to mom) -> Students say "mom" or point. (Pragmatic: Naming family members)</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is she doing?" (pointing to mom cooking) -> Students mimic cooking action. (Specific Objective: Mimic simple actions)</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is dad happy?" (pointing to a happy dad) -> Students nod or say "Yes, happy!" and show a happy face.</li>
        </ul>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">The teacher explicitly repeats "This is my mom, she is happy" or "This is my dad, he is playing." (Grammar: Possessive adjectives, Pronouns)</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Mediation.</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Naming family members (mom, dad, brother, sister), describing basic emotions (happy, sad), and actions (play, live, love, see) using "This is my..." and simple gestures. Grammar: "Who is it?", "This is my..." Pronunciation: /m/ in "mom", /s/ in "sister", /p/ in "play", /b/ in "brother".</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
        <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 1: "Mimic the Family" (Controlled Practice)</b>
            <br>The teacher holds up flashcards of family members. For each card, the teacher says, "This is [Mom]. She is [happy/cooking]." Students repeat "Mom!" and then mimic the action or emotion. (e.g., Mom cooks, Dad plays /p/, Brother jumps /b/, Sister sings /s/). The teacher emphasizes clear articulation of initial sounds and models the connection between words and gestures.</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 2: "My Family Picture Frame" (Semi-controlled)</b>
            <br>Each student receives a simple drawing sheet with an empty frame and pre-drawn stick figures for "mom," "dad," "brother," "sister," "baby." The teacher calls out a family member, e.g., "Draw a happy mom!" Students draw a simple happy face on the 'mom' stick figure. Then, the teacher asks, "Who is this?" and students point to their drawing and say, "My mom!" (Grammar: Possessive adjectives; Pragmatic: Naming family members).</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 3: "Show and Tell a Family Face" (Pair Interaction)</b>
            <br>Students work in pairs. They take turns showing one of their 'My Family Picture Frame' drawings to their partner. Student A points to a drawing (e.g., the mom) and says, "This is my mom." Student B then mimics a happy or sad face according to the drawing and says, "Happy mom!" or "Sad mom!" They switch roles. The teacher circulates, providing support and encouraging simple communication.</li>
        </ul>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "My Family".</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> "My Family Tree" Presentation and Mimicry. Students will present their previously created "My Family Tree" (from the 21st Century Skills Project) to a small group or the class, using their drawings/pictures and gestures to explain who is in their family and how they feel or what they do.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b></p>
        <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Each student brings their "My Family Tree" (with drawings or pictures of mom, dad, brother, sister, baby, etc.).</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher models: "This is my mom. She is happy!" (teacher smiles and points to mom). "This is my dad. He plays!" (teacher claps hands or mimics playing and points to dad).</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students take turns showing their family tree. They point to 2-3 family members (e.g., mom, dad, brother/sister) and use "This is my..." and a word (e.g., "mom," "dad") along with a gesture or facial expression (e.g., "happy," "sad," "play").</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher encourages other students to listen and mimic the gestures shown by the presenter.</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">This activity integrates the learning outcome by having students act out family roles and use gestures to describe, mediating their personal family information through visual and physical representations for peers. (Sociolinguistic: Respecting family roles by sharing).</li>
        </ul>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during the "My Family Tree" presentations.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Each student presents their "My Family Tree" and verbally identifies at least two family members, using "This is my..." and an accompanying gesture/mimicry.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher Checklist):</b></p>
        <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Can clearly point to family members on their family tree.</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Uses "This is my..." to introduce at least two family members (e.g., "This is my mom," "This is my dad."). (Grammar: Possessive adjectives)</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Uses an intelligible pronunciation for "mom" and "dad." (Pronunciation: /m/)</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Mimics a simple action or emotion (e.g., happy, sad, play) for at least one family member. (Specific Objective)</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Attempts to communicate the information to peers.</li>
        </ul>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket (Verbal or Visual):</b></p>
        <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Teacher asks, showing a picture of a child pointing to their family tree) "Can I show my mom and dad?" (Students give a thumbs up or thumbs down).</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Teacher asks) "Did I use my hands to show happy or sad?" (Students give a thumbs up or thumbs down).</li>
        </ul>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
        <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What did I like about sharing my family tree?" (Students can point to a happy face if they liked it, or a sad face if they didn't).</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Who did I talk about?" (Students can try to name one family member).</li>
        </ul>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
        <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in this mediation lesson? The visual aids (picture book, family tree drawings) and TPR were highly engaging for Pre-K. Students enjoyed mimicking actions and showing their personal family trees. The "This is my..." structure was well-retained.</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments and scaffolds are needed for the next lesson or next time this lesson is taught? Some students needed more individual prompting to say "This is my..." and point simultaneously. Providing more opportunities for one-on-one interaction during the practice stage would be beneficial. Integrating a short video showing different family actions could enhance visual input.</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">How does this lesson connect to the next lesson or theme sequence? This lesson concludes the "My Family" theme. The next theme could build on personal identification, perhaps focusing on "My House" or "My Friends," allowing students to continue using "my" and simple descriptive language in new contexts while reinforcing the ability to mediate information about themselves and their immediate surroundings.</li>
        </ul>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>